.TH CCAT 1 "2024-01-15" "ctools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccat \- cat for LLM context windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccat
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-\-color
.IR never | auto | always ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I "agent/session_id"
...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ccat
(cat for context windows) prints one or more conversations, the way
.BR cat (1)
prints a file.
.B cdir
lists sessions and
.BR cgrep
finds them;
.B ccat
shows the actual conversation.
.PP
By default each conversation is printed as a JSON list of
.BR {"role", "content"}
objects (one array per session) \(em the same shape
.BR ccopy
uses on the wire. JSON is the first transportable format for a conversation, so
it is what
.B ccat
hands you and what you pipe onward: to
.BR jq ,
to another tool, or back into a session with
.BR "ccopy \-\-import-json" .
Pass
.B \-t
(\fB--text\fR) for a readable transcript instead: a short role label in a fixed
column, followed by the message content.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text
Print a readable transcript instead of the default JSON.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-raw
Print the lossless envelope: the
.B context
array (the default JSON) plus the source agent's verbatim
.B raw
records and the session metadata
(\fBsource\fR, \fBmodel\fR, \fBcreated\fR, \fBmodified\fR). The session facts sit at the
top of the object, not on every record. Agents whose storage cannot be
introspected omit the \fBraw\fR key; the \fBcontext\fR still carries the conversation.
.TP
.BR \-\-color " MODE"
Colorize the \fB--text\fR transcript.
.IR MODE
is
.BR never ", " auto ", or " always
(default
.BR auto ).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I agent/session_id
A session to print, as
.I agent/session_id
(e.g.
.IR opencode/ses_abc123 ).
Give as many as you like; they print in order, separated by a blank line.
.TP
.I ssh://[user@]host[:port]/agent/session_id
A session on a remote host.
.B ccat
fetches it in two tiers: if the remote runs ctools, its own export streams just
the session's JSON over one ssh; otherwise
.B ccat
pulls the agent's storage over ssh and reads it locally, so a bare remote needs
only
.B sshd
and
.BR tar .
.SH EXAMPLES
Print one conversation (JSON by default):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccat opencode/ses_abc123
[
  {"role": "user", "content": "do a git diff to v0.16.10 ... i broke toolcalling"}
]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print it as a readable transcript:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccat --text opencode/ses_abc123
    you        do a git diff to v0.16.10 ... i broke toolcalling somehow
    assistant  Let me look at the broader context around tool calling...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe the JSON onward:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccat opencode/ses_abc123 | jq .
.fi
.RE
.PP
Dump the lossless envelope and address it with jq:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccat --raw opencode/ses_abc123 | jq .context[0]
{ "role": "user", "content": "do a git diff to v0.16.10 ..." }
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print every session that
.BR cgrep
matched:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep -l llcat opencode | xargs ccat          # as JSON
$ cgrep -l llcat opencode | xargs ccat --text   # as a transcript
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read a conversation from another host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccat ssh://chris@remote/opencode/ses_abc123
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one session was printed.
.TP
.B 1
No session could be printed (bad id, unknown or uninstalled agent, no args).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cdir (1),
.BR cgrep (1),
.BR ccopy (1)
.SH AUTHORS
ctools project.
